<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sándor Petőfi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1823">1823</a>–<a href="/years/1849">1849</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sándor Petrovics, Alexander Petrovics</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sándor Petőfi was a Hungarian poet and liberal revolutionary, born in Kiskőrös in 1823 and considered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>'s national poet. His poems relied on folkloric elements and song-like verse, and he wrote the long fairy-tale epic János Vitéz (1845). On 15 March 1848 he and the Youths of March led the rising in Pest, reading his Nemzeti dal (National <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>) and the 12 Points to the crowds and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> them after declaring an end to censorship. He then fought in the Transylvanian army under Józef Bem and was last seen alive at the Battle of Segesvár on 31 July 1849, where he most likely died; his body was never found.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sandor-petofi.jpg" alt="Sándor Petőfi" width="330" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Soma Orlai Petrich · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Orlai-petofi1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Sándor Petőfi</b>'s poetry was read aloud with the 12 points in Pest.</blockquote><a href="/movement/revolutions-of-1848">Revolutions of 1848 · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The revolutions of 1848 brought Romantic poets into politics: Alphonse de Lamartine directed the foreign policy of the new French republic, and in Pest <b>Sándor Petőfi</b> recited his “National Song” on 15 March.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
